          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Hypertension is not diagnosed from one reading. The guidance I could retrieve gives two thresholds, one from a US source and one from a European source, and these differ. I could not retrieve NICE or WHO thresholds, so I can't compare them.\n\n**What the retrieved guidance says** (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, a major cardiology society consensus summary):\n\n- **US convention (office readings):** Normal is below 120/80 mm Hg. Elevated is 120 to 129 systolic with diastolic below 80. Stage 1 is 130 to 139 systolic or 80 to 89 diastolic. Stage 2 is 140 or higher systolic, or 90 or higher diastolic. If the two numbers fall in different categories, the higher one applies.\n- **Confirmation:** A diagnosis needs an average of two or more readings on two or more separate occasions. Home or 24-hour monitoring can reveal white-coat hypertension (high in clinic, normal elsewhere) and masked hypertension (the reverse).\n- **European convention:** Office hypertension begins at 140/90 mm Hg. The 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 band is labeled \"high-normal\" rather than stage 1.\n\n**Where the bodies differ:** The same reading gets a different label depending on which convention is used, and the number of people counted as hypertensive changes substantially. The guidance also notes that the two conventions lead to different treatment advice for low-risk stage 1 cases. In the US convention these people are often offered treatment, while the European approach often advises lifestyle changes alone.\n\n**Where they agree:** The retrieved sources do not state a point of agreement on thresholds. They do agree that diagnosis rests on repeated readings, not a single one.\n\n**What I could not establish:**\n- The retrieved material does not give NICE or WHO thresholds. I have not filled these in from memory.\n- It does not name the specific bodies behind the US and European summaries, so I cannot confirm which document is ACC/AHA or ESC/ESH.\n- It does not describe how strong the evidence is behind either threshold. I can't rate that from these passages.\n\nIf you need the NICE or WHO position, or the evidence behind each threshold, those would need separate retrieval. A clinician can also tell you which convention applies to a specific reading.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
